Crisp Apple a handful of almonds and a  pint of blueberries all have one thing  in common  they've probably got here because of a  bee and according to researchers their  pollination is worth fifteen billion  dollars to the US Agriculture industry  each year but over the past decade bees  have been dying at an alarming rate and  nobody knows why so here's the situation  researchers began tracking bees in 2006  since then beekeepers have reported  losing an average of 30% of their bees  in the winters for a rough reference  beekeepers estimate that before 2007 the  rate was 15% what's more today  beekeepers are losing bees in the summer  when bee should be thriving in the past  two years  summer losses have been equal to or  greater than winter losses and annual  losses reach 44% in 2015 but what do  these losses actually affect let's take  a look at almonds California produces  80% of the world's almonds to pollinate  almond trees farmers rent bee hives 30%  of all hive rentals in the US go to  almond orchards from 1995 to 2010 the  price of hive rentals triple half of  that cost increase can be attributed to  the loss of bees environmentalists are  pointing the finger squarely on a class  of pesticides called neonicotinoids but  companies say the insecticides are  unfairly targeted and ensuing studies  have drawn conflicting conclusions one  thing scientists agree on however are  that multiple factors are stressing bees  out including chemical resistant  parasitic mites B viruses and poor  nutrition now here's the argument on one  side of the fence are those who oppose  pesticide restriction they say that the  loss of bees has not significantly  affected the price of food they argue  bans will reduce crop yields and point  to the European Union which recently  restricted some neonicotinoids in an  effort to save bees farmers in the UK  saw their rapeseed harvest fall 15% last  year  on the other side are those who see  industrial farming directly contributing  to the plight of the honeybee they  advocate for fewer pesticides and for  measures like more crop rotation to  improve bee nutrition and get colonies  back on track
 

